WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.420
Let u be odd and greater than one, and
let I be its distinct prime divisors.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:10.740
For each subset S, multiply its chosen
prime roots to obtain zeta sub S.

00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:14.360
The empty subset gives one. At that input,

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:20.260
let r sub S be d divided by c,
and w sub S be g divided by c.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:27.300
Use one ring A: the integers with i, the
chosen roots, and one over u adjoined.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.100
The norm identities make every evaluated
polynomial a unit in A. In particular,

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:38.320
the inverse of c at zeta sub S is its
complex conjugate divided by u squared.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.840
Thus all these ratios belong
to the same ring and are units.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.720
Node six gives an exact alternating
product of the r ratios equal to one.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:52.780
An even subset contributes its
ratio with exponent positive one.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.530
An odd subset contributes the
inverse, with exponent negative one.

00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:01.400
Move the even factors above a fraction
bar and the odd factors below it.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:03.540
This is the same exact product.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:07.400
Now multiply both sides
by the odd-subset product.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:11.460
It is a product of units.
The denominator cancels,

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:15.140
leaving the even-subset product
equal to the odd-subset product.

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.240
This equality holds in
A, before reduction.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:20.900
All factors now have positive exponents.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:25.520
For u equal to three, there
are just two subsets: empty,

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.780
and the subset containing three.
The product equality is simply r

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:34.250
at the empty subset equal to r
at the subset containing three.

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.690
For u equal to fifteen, the two
distinct primes are three and five.

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.440
There are four subsets. The even
subsets are empty and the pair three,

00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:47.500
five; the odd subsets
are the two singletons.

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:52.800
The exact equality is r at the
empty subset times r at the pair,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.360
equal to r at three times r at
five. This constrains two products.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.060
It does not require all four
individual ratios to coincide.

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:05.780
Keep the product identity
for the general argument.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:11.160
The w ratios obey the same exact
alternating-product identity.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:14.860
Move their odd-subset factors
across in the same way.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.180
Their even-subset product equals
their odd-subset product, again in A.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:23.860
Keep both equalities together.
We now have finite products with

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:27.400
only positive exponents, built
from the same evaluation ring.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:32.000
The next step can expand each side
directly and compare the linear terms.
